% Regression: holding a form's description reaches for machinery by name
% -- the recorder a drawing is taken by, the placement that puts one on
% the page, and the replay that paints one into a device that paints.
% Every one of those is a dot-named operator relocated out of systemdict
% into .internaldict, and a name that cannot be found there is undefined
% at the first form rather than at load. This runs a form twice, so both
% the taking and the placing are reached, and either failing is an error
% rather than a page that quietly took the other path.
%
% Run through the ppm device by run-ps-test.sh, and it has to be a device
% a drawing can be held for: one declaring the five marking methods, a
% colour space a record holds, no path filling and no screen. A run on a
% device that paints nothing takes the uncached path and reaches none of
% what this exists to guard. Prints SUCCESS iff a cacheable form runs
% through execform without error.

/faildict 1 dict def faildict /n 0 put
/assert { % bool (name) . -
    exch { pop }{ (FAIL: )print print (\n)print faildict /n 2 copy get 1 add put } ifelse
} bind def

/F <<
    /FormType 1
    /BBox [0 0 120 120]
    /Matrix [1 0 0 1 0 0]
    /PaintProc {
        pop
        0 0 120 120 rectfill
        1 setgray 20 20 80 80 rectfill
    }
>> def

% execform the same form twice: the first pass populates the form cache (the
% path that builds the raster device and clears it with the rect-fill
% operators), the second pass replays from the cache. Neither may error.
{ gsave F execform grestore
  gsave F execform grestore } stopped not
    (a cacheable form runs through execform without error) assert

faildict /n get 0 eq { (SUCCESS\n) print } { faildict /n get =print ( failures\n) print } ifelse
flush
quit
